																					# ABSTRACTION IN JAVA

1.) 		DEFINITION OF ABSTRACTION

			---

			------>	Abstraction in Java is the process of hiding internal implementation details and showing only the essential functionality to the user.
					It focuses on WHAT an object does rather than HOW it does it.




2.)			 ABSTRACT KEYWORD

---

			Java provides the "abstract" keyword to achieve abstraction.

					The abstract keyword can be used with:

								1. Abstract Class
												2. Abstract Method

		## 2.1) ABSTRACT METHOD

										An abstract method is a method that provides only the method declaration without implementation.


					Example:

							abstract void exam(int minInternals, String syllabus,
 
 
 							int range, String duration);

				Important:

							An abstract method does NOT have a method body.

					Example:

								abstract void exam();

							There is no { } after the method declaration.

				## 2.2) EXAMPLE

					abstract class JNTUK
					{
						abstract void exam(int minInternals, String syllabus,
								int range, String duration);
																}

				Here:

						abstract  -> keyword
						void      -> return type
						exam      -> method name
						(...)     -> parameters

						The abstract method only tells WHAT needs to be done.
						It does not tell HOW it should be done.

				## 2.3) CHILD CLASS

						class GIET extends JNTUK
						{
							void exam(int minInternals, String syllabus,
								int range, String duration)
							{
								int externalMarks = 50;
								int total = minInternals + externalMarks;


    							System.out.println("Total Marks = " + total);
									}
```

									}

				Here GIET provides the implementation of the abstract exam() method.


													FLOW:

																JNTUK
																	|
																	| abstract exam()
																	|
																	v
																GIET
																	|
	                                                				| exam() implementation
																	v
																	Implementation



						## 3.2) ONLY CONCRETE METHODS: 

														A concrete method is a normal method that has a body.
														
														abstract class JNTUK
														{
														void attendance()
														{
														System.out.println("Attendance is required");
														}
														
														```
														void timetable()
														{
														    System.out.println("Exam timetable");
														}
														```
														
														}
													
			## 3.3) BOTH ABSTRACT AND CONCRETE METHODS:-
													
													abstract class JNTUK
													{
													abstract void exam();
													
													```
													void attendance()
													{
													    System.out.println("Attendance is required");
													}
													```
													
													}
													
													Here:
													
													exam()       -> Abstract method
													attendance() -> Concrete method

		4. IMPORTANT POINTS TO REMEMBER:--
											
											---
											
											1. Abstraction means hiding implementation details.
											
											2. It shows only essential functionality to the user.
											
											3. Abstraction focuses on WHAT, not HOW.
											
											4. Java uses the abstract keyword for abstraction.
											
											5. An abstract method has no implementation/body.
											
											6. The child class provides the implementation of
											   the abstract method.
											
											7. An abstract class can contain:
											   a) Only abstract methods
											   b) Only concrete methods
											   c) Both abstract and concrete methods
											
											8. An abstract class can have variables.
											
											9. An abstract class can have constructors.
											
											10. We cannot directly create an object of an abstract class.
											
											11. A concrete child class must implement all abstract methods.

				5) EASY WAY TO REMEMBER:-

												ABSTRACTION = WHAT TO DO, NOT HOW TO DO IT.
												
												Example:
												
												abstract void exam();
												
										WHAT?
												-> Exam method must be available.
												
										HOW?
												-> The child class decides how the exam method works.
